- How would you test this with mixed-language prompts?
- How would latency constraints influence the design?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a modular, latency-aware prompt routing flow in an edge/cloud context. It requires turning a user goal into a module selection, a concise execution plan, and a concrete payload while considering safety and edge cases.

## Key Concepts
- Prompt routing and modular design
- PII redaction and latency constraints
- Edge-case handling and fallbacks

## Code Example
```javascript
// Tiny router sketch
function routePrompt(req){
  const intent = req.intent||'';
  const data = req.data_sensitivity||'';
  const latency = req.latency_budget||Infinity;
  if(intent.includes('summary') && data==='PII' && latency<=1000){
    return {module:'Summary-Builder', plan:['fetch','redact','summarize'], payload:{regions:req.regions||['us-east','us-west'], redact:true}};
  }
  return {module:'Data-Extractor', plan:['extract'], payload:{}};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test this for latency budgets and safety guards?
- How would you extend to handle parallel modules and partial results?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design multi-tenant prompt systems, policy-driven routing, and drift auditing; checks comfort with latency budgeting and audit trails; reveals understanding of safety constraints.

## Key Concepts
- Multi-tenant policy blobs
- Model routing via data_classifications
- Safety guard injection preserving intent
- Drift detection and latency budgeting
- Audit log design and replayability

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal prototype illustrating routing and rewriting
function routePrompt(prompt, tenantPolicy, models) {
  // decide avatar
  const avatar = tenantPolicy.data_classifications.includes('medical') ? models.avatarA : models.avatarB;
  // rewrite to inject guard
  const guarded = `Guarded: ${prompt} [guard:${tenantPolicy.allowed_actions.join(',')}]`;
  // fake latency
  return { guarded, avatar: avatar.name };
}
```

